<!-- SECTION 6: STRATEGIC RECOMMENDATIONS -->
<div class="section" id="recommendations">
  <h2>6. Strategic Recommendations</h2>
  <p>Each recommendation below is grounded in a specific data finding from this analysis.</p>

  <h3>R1. Prioritize Reskilling for Business Analysts and Data Analysts</h3>
  <div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Data Finding:</strong> Business Analysts (0.75 avg risk, 43% survival) and Data Analysts (0.65 avg risk, 57% survival) face the highest automation pressure. These are the only roles with 0% strong-survival records.
  </div>
  <p><strong>Action:</strong> Launch targeted reskilling programs to transition these workers toward Python-centric data science, cloud engineering, or cybersecurity pathways. The data shows that moving primary skill from Excel/SQL to Python alone raises survival rates from 43&ndash;57% to 99.9%. Prioritize workers with 3+ years of tenure who have institutional knowledge worth preserving.</p>

  <h3>R2. Expand Investment in AI Research, ML Engineering, and Cybersecurity</h3>
  <div class="callout success">
    <strong>Data Finding:</strong> AI Researcher, ML Engineer, and Cybersecurity Analyst maintain 100% survival rates and the highest salary premiums ($42.8K, $39.3K, $27.7K median). Deep Learning and Security skills show the strongest survival profiles.
  </div>
  <p><strong>Action:</strong> Increase headcount and compensation budgets for these three roles. The combination of low risk, high survival, and growing demand makes them the safest long-term talent investments. Consider creating internal "AI Centers of Excellence" staffed primarily by AI Researchers and ML Engineers.</p>

  <h3>R3. Monitor Software Engineers for Emerging Risk</h3>
  <div class="callout warning">
    <strong>Data Finding:</strong> Software Engineers have a current risk score of 0.45 (Medium) that will reach 0.55 by 2035. While their survival rate is 97.4%, their strong-survival rate is only 20.5% &mdash; far below similarly-scored roles.
  </div>
  <p><strong>Action:</strong> Invest in upskilling Software Engineers toward cloud, DevOps, and ML competencies. The data suggests that generalist coding skills are becoming increasingly automatable, but engineers who layer on cloud (AWS/Docker) or ML specialization can transition into roles with much stronger survival profiles.</p>

  <h3>R4. Address India's Compound Risk with Market-Specific Strategy</h3>
  <div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Data Finding:</strong> India has the lowest survival rate (78.6%), lowest strong-survival rate (8.8%), and lowest median salary ($12,448). This compound disadvantage makes Indian workforce segments particularly vulnerable.
  </div>
  <p><strong>Action:</strong> Develop India-specific reskilling initiatives focused on high-survival skills (Python, Deep Learning, Security). Consider adjusting hiring profiles in India toward AI-resilient roles. For existing India-based Business Analyst and Data Analyst teams, accelerate migration toward data science and ML engineering functions.</p>

  <h3>R5. De-Emphasize Education-Based Hiring; Emphasize Skill-Based Assessment</h3>
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Data Finding:</strong> Education level shows virtually no correlation with salary (Bachelor: $31,569 vs. PhD: $31,672) or survival rate (89.3% vs. 89.7%). In contrast, primary skill creates massive survival divergences (Deep Learning: 100% vs. Excel: 43%).
  </div>
  <p><strong>Action:</strong> Shift hiring and promotion criteria from degree requirements to demonstrated skill portfolios. Invest in skills-based assessment tools and internal skill taxonomies that map directly to the resilience tiers identified in this analysis.</p>

  <h3>R6. Build Workforce Planning Around Role Risk Tiers, Not Seniority</h3>
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Data Finding:</strong> Experience level has negligible impact on AI risk (Entry: 0.396 vs. Senior: 0.393). The role itself is the dominant risk driver, with a 0.60-point spread between AI Researcher (0.15) and Business Analyst (0.75).
  </div>
  <p><strong>Action:</strong> Redesign workforce planning models to segment by role-risk tier rather than experience band. Allocate reskilling budgets proportionally to role risk scores rather than distributing evenly across the organization.</p>
</div>

<!-- DATA NOTE -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>Data Notes &amp; Methodology</h2>
  <p><strong>Source:</strong> Kaggle synthetic dataset (shree0910/ai-job-risk-and-salary-dataset-20152035), 12,343 records across 13 columns. The data is synthetically generated using realistic assumptions inspired by global job market patterns, AI adoption rates, and salary distributions.</p>
  <p><strong>Time Horizon:</strong> 2015&ndash;2024 represent historical-analog periods; 2025&ndash;2035 represent simulated projections.</p>
  <p><strong>Key Assumptions:</strong> AI risk scores increase linearly at +0.01 per year per role. Salary values are in USD and do not account for purchasing power parity. Skill demand scores represent a relative index (0&ndash;100) reflecting market demand.</p>
  <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> As a synthetic dataset, findings should inform directional thinking rather than precision forecasting. Geographic salary comparisons do not adjust for cost of living. The linear risk progression may not capture potential step-function disruptions from breakthrough AI capabilities.</p>
</div>

